  rule whose licence is not on its short admitted list.

## Why redistribution in the npm package is allowed

- LGPL-2.1 section 1 allows verbatim copies "in any medium" with the copyright
  and licence notices kept and a copy of the licence supplied. Section 2 allows
  modified copies that carry a prominent modification notice and stay under the
  LGPL. `rules.yaml` is modified only by concatenation and by prefixing each
  rule id with its upstream path; its header says so.
- The Commons Clause withholds one right: to "Sell" the Software, meaning to
  provide to third parties, for a fee or other consideration, a product or
  service whose value derives entirely or substantially from the Software.
  pi-lens is free, MIT and not sold; its value is not derived from this pack.
  A downstream party that sells a product built substantially on this pack is
  bound by that condition.
- LGPL-2.1 section 2 ("mere aggregation"): shipping the pack beside pi-lens does
  not bring pi-lens under the LGPL. Opengrep reads the pack as data.

## The refreshed pack is not this pack

pi-lens refreshes the registry rule pack (`https://semgrep.dev/c/auto`) onto
the user's own disk. Those rules are under the Semgrep Rules License v1.0, which
does not allow redistribution ("This license does not allow you to distribute
the rules"), so they are never copied into this package or into pi-lens's
repository. The download runs on the user's machine, as `--config auto` did.

## Rebuilding

`node scripts/build-opengrep-pack.mjs --upstream <checkout at the commit above>
--check` rebuilds `rules.yaml` and `manifest.json` from the rule ids already in
the pack and exits 1 unless the output is byte-identical.
